1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:04,432
A trip app says Aarav's ride
averaged twelve kilometres per hour.

2
00:00:04,432 --> 00:00:09,014
His speedometer showed twenty five
on the slope, and zero at the signal.

3
00:00:09,414 --> 00:00:10,893
Three different numbers.

4
00:00:10,893 --> 00:00:12,435
And all three are true.

5
00:00:12,885 --> 00:00:14,335
So Aarav wonders.

6
00:00:14,335 --> 00:00:16,782
Which one is his real velocity?

7
00:00:17,132 --> 00:00:18,776
It depends on one thing.

8
00:00:18,776 --> 00:00:21,191
A whole trip, or one single instant?

9
00:00:21,691 --> 00:00:25,606
By the end of this video, you will
find the velocity at one instant.

10
00:00:26,256 --> 00:00:27,761
Start with the whole trip.

11
00:00:28,111 --> 00:00:32,286
Average velocity is the displacement
divided by the time taken.

12
00:00:32,686 --> 00:00:35,956
Aarav's college is three
kilometres east of his home.

13
00:00:35,956 --> 00:00:38,136
He gets there in fifteen minutes.

14
00:00:38,536 --> 00:00:41,830
Three kilometres divided
by a quarter of an hour.

15
00:00:41,830 --> 00:00:44,168
Twelve kilometres per hour, east.

16
00:00:44,618 --> 00:00:47,154
But it hides the stops and the fast bits.

17
00:00:47,154 --> 00:00:49,038
One number for the whole trip.

18
00:00:49,688 --> 00:00:53,187
Now let's zoom into five
seconds on our motion notebook.

19
00:00:53,537 --> 00:00:55,957
Aarav starts from rest at the signal.

20
00:00:55,957 --> 00:00:59,022
His position is x equals
t squared, in metres.

21
00:00:59,422 --> 00:01:02,872
Every second he covers more
ground than the second before.

22
00:01:02,872 --> 00:01:04,621
So the graph curves upward.

23
00:01:05,021 --> 00:01:06,509
Slow motion replay.

24
00:01:06,509 --> 00:01:08,839
Freeze at t equals two seconds.

25
00:01:09,339 --> 00:01:12,453
How fast is he going at
exactly this instant?

26
00:01:13,103 --> 00:01:14,307
Try an average.

27
00:01:14,307 --> 00:01:18,029
From two seconds to four
seconds he moves twelve metres.

28
00:01:18,029 --> 00:01:19,651
Six metres per second.

29
00:01:20,101 --> 00:01:22,132
Shrink the gap to one second.

30
00:01:22,132 --> 00:01:23,650
He moves five metres.

31
00:01:23,650 --> 00:01:25,267
Five metres per second.

32
00:01:25,717 --> 00:01:26,809
Half a second.

33
00:01:26,809 --> 00:01:29,041
Four point five metres per second.

34
00:01:29,491 --> 00:01:31,039
A tenth of a second.

35
00:01:31,039 --> 00:01:32,218
Four point one.

36
00:01:32,668 --> 00:01:34,437
A hundredth of a second.

37
00:01:34,437 --> 00:01:35,942
Four point zero one.

38
00:01:35,942 --> 00:01:38,024
The answers close in on four.

39
00:01:38,474 --> 00:01:42,716
On the graph, the chord turns into
a line that just touches the curve.

40
00:01:42,716 --> 00:01:43,616
The tangent.

41
00:01:44,244 --> 00:01:47,069
Let's write this as a
rule, one step at a time.

42
00:01:47,419 --> 00:01:48,387
Step one.

43
00:01:48,387 --> 00:01:52,758
Average velocity, v bar,
equals delta x over delta t.

44
00:01:53,508 --> 00:01:54,441
Step two.

45
00:01:54,441 --> 00:01:56,956
Let delta t shrink towards zero.

46
00:01:56,956 --> 00:02:01,839
The velocity at an instant is the
limit of delta x over delta t.

47
00:02:02,589 --> 00:02:03,603
Step three.

48
00:02:03,603 --> 00:02:06,293
This limit is written d x by d t.

49
00:02:06,293 --> 00:02:09,400
How fast the position changes, right now.

50
00:02:10,150 --> 00:02:11,093
Step four.

51
00:02:11,093 --> 00:02:14,703
For x equals t squared,
d x by d t is two t.

52
00:02:14,703 --> 00:02:17,651
At two seconds, four metres per second.

53
00:02:18,401 --> 00:02:19,485
Remember this.

54
00:02:19,485 --> 00:02:22,239
The chord's slope is the average velocity.

55
00:02:22,239 --> 00:02:25,519
The tangent's slope is the
velocity at an instant.

56
00:02:26,219 --> 00:02:27,429
Side by side.

57
00:02:27,429 --> 00:02:30,354
Average velocity needs a whole interval.

58
00:02:30,354 --> 00:02:32,931
Total displacement over total time.

59
00:02:33,331 --> 00:02:36,681
Instantaneous velocity
needs only one instant.

60
00:02:36,681 --> 00:02:38,984
A speedometer shows its size.

61
00:02:39,584 --> 00:02:40,645
Now you try.

62
00:02:40,645 --> 00:02:45,424
Aarav rides three hundred metres to
a shop and straight back home, in ten

63
00:02:45,424 --> 00:02:46,324
minutes.

64
00:02:46,155 --> 00:02:48,215
What is his average velocity?

65
00:02:48,215 --> 00:02:49,534
Pause and decide.

66
00:02:52,734 --> 00:02:53,634
Zero!

67
00:02:53,317 --> 00:02:57,115
He ends where he started,
so his displacement is zero.

68
00:02:57,115 --> 00:03:01,726
His average speed is one metre per
second, but his average velocity is

69
00:03:01,726 --> 00:03:02,626
zero.

70
00:03:02,959 --> 00:03:06,254
Now read velocities straight
off a position time graph.

71
00:03:06,604 --> 00:03:10,741
Where the curve climbs steeply,
the velocity is large and positive.

72
00:03:11,141 --> 00:03:14,102
Where it slopes down,
the velocity is negative.

73
00:03:14,102 --> 00:03:15,396
He is coming back.

74
00:03:15,796 --> 00:03:16,696
Your turn.

75
00:03:16,602 --> 00:03:20,575
At the very top of the hump, the
graph is flat for an instant.

76
00:03:20,575 --> 00:03:22,334
What is the velocity there?

77
00:03:22,334 --> 00:03:23,473
Pause and think.

78
00:03:26,673 --> 00:03:27,573
Zero!

79
00:03:27,227 --> 00:03:30,903
The tangent at the top is
flat, so its slope is zero.

80
00:03:30,903 --> 00:03:32,425
He is turning around.

81
00:03:33,075 --> 00:03:34,811
Now, one worked example.

82
00:03:35,161 --> 00:03:40,053
A cyclist moves so that x equals
four t squared plus two t, in metres.

83
00:03:40,053 --> 00:03:44,667
Find the average velocity in the
first two seconds, and the velocity at

84
00:03:44,667 --> 00:03:45,666
two seconds.

85
00:03:46,166 --> 00:03:48,598
At t equals zero, x is zero.

86
00:03:48,598 --> 00:03:51,838
At t equals two, x is sixteen plus four.

87
00:03:51,838 --> 00:03:53,038
Twenty metres.

88
00:03:53,788 --> 00:03:57,170
Average velocity is twenty
metres over two seconds.

89
00:03:57,170 --> 00:03:58,809
Ten metres per second.

90
00:03:59,559 --> 00:04:02,132
For the instant, find d x by d t.

91
00:04:02,132 --> 00:04:04,067
v equals eight t plus two.

92
00:04:04,817 --> 00:04:07,819
At two seconds, v is
eighteen metres per second.

93
00:04:07,819 --> 00:04:11,186
Almost double the average,
because he keeps speeding up.

94
00:04:11,836 --> 00:04:13,302
Now, the exam trap.

95
00:04:13,652 --> 00:04:18,162
Aarav rides six kilometres at ten
kilometres per hour, then six more at

96
00:04:18,162 --> 00:04:19,062
fifteen.

97
00:04:18,877 --> 00:04:22,601
Many students take the mean,
and say twelve point five.

98
00:04:23,001 --> 00:04:23,901
Wrong.

99
00:04:23,615 --> 00:04:26,211
He spends more time on the slow half.

100
00:04:26,211 --> 00:04:28,273
Twelve kilometres in one hour.

101
00:04:28,273 --> 00:04:30,124
Twelve kilometres per hour.

102
00:04:30,774 --> 00:04:32,312
Where is this asked?

103
00:04:32,312 --> 00:04:33,686
Usually like this.

104
00:04:33,686 --> 00:04:38,477
Given x as a function of t, find the
average velocity over an interval,

105
00:04:38,477 --> 00:04:40,759
and the velocity at one instant.

106
00:04:41,359 --> 00:04:42,601
Exam level now.

107
00:04:42,601 --> 00:04:46,393
x equals t cubed, minus
three t squared, plus four.

108
00:04:46,393 --> 00:04:50,996
Find the average velocity from zero
to three seconds, and the velocity

109
00:04:50,996 --> 00:04:52,344
at three seconds.

110
00:04:52,344 --> 00:04:53,640
Pause and solve.

111
00:04:59,040 --> 00:05:00,349
The answer is A.

112
00:05:00,349 --> 00:05:02,745
Zero, and nine metres per second.

113
00:05:03,245 --> 00:05:04,764
x at zero is four.

114
00:05:04,764 --> 00:05:08,965
x at three is twenty seven,
minus twenty seven, plus four.

115
00:05:08,965 --> 00:05:09,877
Also four.

116
00:05:09,877 --> 00:05:12,192
So the average velocity is zero.

117
00:05:12,692 --> 00:05:14,004
Now differentiate.

118
00:05:14,004 --> 00:05:16,668
v equals three t squared minus six t.

119
00:05:16,668 --> 00:05:19,839
At three seconds, twenty
seven minus eighteen.

120
00:05:19,839 --> 00:05:21,532
Nine metres per second.

121
00:05:22,132 --> 00:05:23,591
Here is your recap.

122
00:05:23,591 --> 00:05:25,090
Screenshot this card.

123
00:05:28,290 --> 00:05:31,127
Can you answer these three
questions without help?

124
00:05:31,527 --> 00:05:32,427
One.

125
00:05:32,067 --> 00:05:35,443
Why can a round trip have
zero average velocity?

126
00:05:36,343 --> 00:05:37,243
Two.

127
00:05:36,903 --> 00:05:41,186
Does a speedometer show an
average or an instantaneous value?

128
00:05:42,086 --> 00:05:42,986
Three.

129
00:05:42,698 --> 00:05:47,348
How do you read the velocity at one
instant from a position time graph?

130
00:05:48,448 --> 00:05:52,344
Next time: velocity tells
how fast position changes.

131
00:05:52,344 --> 00:05:55,378
So what tells how fast velocity changes?

132
00:05:55,378 --> 00:05:58,150
Average and instantaneous acceleration.
